<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marikana massacre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2012">2012</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Marikana massacre took place on 16 August 2012 near the Lonmin platinum mine at Marikana in North West Province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, when police opened fire on striking miners, killing 34. Ten people, including police officers, had been killed in the preceding days. It was the deadliest use of force by South African security forces since the end of apartheid, and a commission of inquiry criticised the police.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marikana-massacre.jpg" alt="Marikana massacre" width="320" height="266" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JMK · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lonmin-platinummyn,_a,_Marikana,_Noordwes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The strike took place against rivalry between the National Union of Mineworkers (NUM), the largest affiliate of the Congress of South African Trade Unions, and the Association of Mineworkers and Construction Union (AMCU), founded by Joseph Mathunjwa after he fell out with the NUM in 1998. The NUM's popularity had begun to decline, partly because of its perceived closeness to government and management. The strike was driven by rock drill operators, about 4,100 of whom Lonmin employed, who worked in some of the most dangerous conditions underground. They sought a rise from R4,000 to R12,500 a month, although the collective wage agreement ran until 2013.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>After a mass meeting on 8 August 2012 at which NUM leaders refused to support the demand, the rock drillers began an unauthorised strike on 10 August. On 11 August, according to strikers and other witnesses, NUM representatives opened fire on the strikers as they marched on the union's office. The police presence grew from 209 officers on 13 August to 718 on 16 August, and between 12 and 14 August at least ten people were killed, including two police officers and two Lonmin security officers.</p><p>On the morning of 16 August the police command referred to the day as &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normandy-landings">D-Day</a>&quot;, and a police spokesman said the gathering on a koppie near the mine would be ended. When police moved in with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbed-wire">barbed wire</a> and Nyala vehicles, officers, who said they were under attack, shot strikers at two sites; 17 were fatally wounded at each, and 78 were injured. Afterwards 270 miners were arrested and charged with the murder of their colleagues under the common purpose doctrine, charges later dropped after public outcry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The strike ended on 18 September with a wage agreement giving increases averaging 11 to 22 per cent; wildcat strikes spread to other mines, and President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacob-zuma">Jacob Zuma</a> sent the army to the platinum belt. The Farlam Commission, set up in August 2012 and chaired by the retired judge Ian Farlam, found the decisive cause of the deaths to be the decision of 15 August to enter a tactical phase to disarm and disperse the miners; the plan was prepared in haste and could not be carried out without significant bloodshed.</p><p>It also found that some shootings at the first scene were in self-defence, but that some of the dead had presented no threat. It criticised the strikers, unions and mine management as well. It made no findings on the second scene, where it said events were a chaotic free for all. Phiyega, named in the report, was suspended with full pay. Farlam lamented in 2022 that no disciplinary proceedings had followed, and only a handful of police officers had been charged, none for the deaths of 16 August.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marikana massacre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…constitutional democracy with regular elections, a free press and an independent judiciary, but it has struggled with corruption, notably the ‘state capture’ under Jacob Zuma investigated by the Zondo Commission, with the police killing of 34 striking miners at <b>Marikana</b> in 2012, and with crime and slow growth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
